course: dictionary
lesson: 14270
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Nipping comes from the verb nip, and one word can mean different things. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning to use. «The puppy kept nipping my fingers, but it did not hurt much.» Puppy and fingers tell you nipping means giving a quick, small bite.
explain: «A nipping wind blew across the beach, so we pulled our coats tight.» Here nipping does not mean biting a person. The wind is cold and stinging, and pulling your coat tight is what you do when it is cold.
explain: «I'm just nipping to the shop, back in five minutes.» Nipping means going somewhere quickly for a short time. This is informal British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand English, so you may hear it more in those places.
fact: Nipping is the -ing form of the verb nip, so it can describe something happening now.
fact: «Nipping» means giving a quick, small bite or pinch.
fact: «Nipping» can describe a cold wind or frost that bites or stings.
fact: In informal English, «nipping out» means going somewhere quickly for a short time.
fact: «Nip in the bud» means stopping something before it grows bigger.
exercise: «The puppy kept nipping my fingers, but it did not hurt much.» What does nipping mean here? | *Giving a quick, small bite | Barking loudly at the postman | Running very fast | Sleeping under the sofa
exercise: «A nipping wind blew across the beach, so we pulled our coats tight.» What does a nipping wind feel like? | *Very cold and stinging | Warm and gentle | Full of sand and noise | Wet and soft
exercise: Dad says, «I'm just nipping to the shop, back in five minutes.» What does nipping mean? | Stopping something before it grows bigger | Walking very slowly | *Going somewhere quickly for a short time | Pinching something very hard
test: Which phrase means stopping a problem before it grows bigger? | nipping wind | nip out | *nip it in the bud | nip and tuck
test: «The two runners were nip and tuck all the way to the finish.» What does nip and tuck mean? | *Very close, neck and neck | Happy and proud | Far behind the others | Tired and sleepy
exam: Which sentence uses nipping to mean going out for a short time? | «A nipping breeze made my ears cold.» | «The puppy was nipping at my shoes.» | «The crab was nipping my toes.» | *«She was nipping out to post a letter.»
